From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Can Us <me@canus.dev>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Pace vsync-less presents on a thread of the SwapChain

Frame generation (0009, 0013) paced its presents in Renderer::endFrame on
the render thread: without vsync the waits between the presents of one
rendered frame stretched the frame itself, so the presented frame rate
stayed at the rendering rate. Sleeping with Windows' 15.6 ms timer tick
also made the spacing uneven.

A Vulkan SwapChain created with CONFIG_DISABLE_VSYNC now presents from its
own thread (VulkanPresentPacer): the driver thread queues each image with
the fence of the command buffer that drew it and its slot among the
presents of one rendered frame (setPresentPacing, set by the renderer before
each commit); the thread waits for the GPU and spaces the presents evenly
over the measured rendered-frame interval with a high-resolution timer,
while the render and driver threads go on with the next frame. Queue access
(submits, presents, wait-idle, and the acquire of a vsync-less swap chain,
which polls instead of blocking) is serialised by fvkutils::queueMutex();
the queue is drained before a swap chain is recreated or destroyed and by
finish(). Vsync-less surfaces get up to seven more images. The present
times the thread records (1024 kept) are what Renderer::getPresentTimes()
returns on Vulkan (getPresentTimes, isPresentPacingSupported). Also clamps
the clear pass viewport of the frame-generation presents to the view (an
unbounded viewport is invalid on a real swap chain). The other backends
keep pacing on the render thread.
---
